{"0": "Q2 2026 revenue came in at KRW 504.8 billion, up 20.2% quarter-on-quarter and up 14.3% year-on-year. Operating profit also surged to KRW 35.5 billion, up 197.4% quarter-on-quarter and up 407.6% year-on-year, marking a recovery from the operating loss in Q4 2025 and extending profitability to two consecutive quarters. The profitability weakness that had persisted through Q1 2026 appears to have eased in Q2.", "1": "Revenue at Simwon North America, the Texas-based subsidiary of MS Autotech's affiliate Myungshin Industry, rose 44% year-on-year in Q2 2026 on increased customer production. Reports also noted that investor sentiment toward Myungshin Industry was stimulated following Tesla's demonstration of its new Roadster in October 2026. If orders related to Tesla's new models materialize, this could be a positive variable for the group's overall performance.", "2": "The stock is trading at a price-to-book ratio below 1x, indicating a discount relative to total shareholders' equity. The company has reportedly taken various financial measures to secure cash, including leveraging subsidiary equity stakes and disposing of non-core assets. If improvements in the financial structure become visible, this could change the multiple at which the stock trades relative to net asset value.", "3": "Both revenue and operating profit declined for two consecutive years in 2024 and 2025 after peaking in 2023. In 2025, the company posted a net loss attributable to controlling shareholders of KRW 108.6 billion and a net loss of KRW 74.9 billion, turning to losses. The operating margin also fell from 8.4% in 2023 to 1.8% in 2025, indicating a weakened profitability base.", "4": "Net losses attributable to controlling shareholders continued for five consecutive quarters from Q2 2025 through Q2 2026. In Q2 2026, the loss widened again compared to the previous quarter. Cumulative losses over the most recent four quarters reached KRW 63.6 billion, suggesting that improvement in the financial structure may take time.", "5": "The debt-to-equity ratio at the end of 2025 rose again to 166.2% compared to the previous year. The company has shown reliance on external funding, including pursuing the issuance of KRW 54 billion in exchangeable bonds in April 2026. Since rising financial costs were cited as one of the causes of the deteriorating 2025 results, additional interest burden could constrain the pace of profitability recovery.", "6": "The debt-to-equity ratio stood at 166.2% at the end of 2025, and the company has reportedly raised funds through private placement bonds and exchangeable bonds. Operating cash flow has been on a declining trend, falling from KRW 291.6 billion in 2023 to KRW 104.9 billion in 2025. As rising financial costs have been cited as one of the factors behind the deteriorating performance, the burden from additional borrowing or changes in the interest rate environment could persist.", "7": "Myungshin Industry, the subsidiary handling the hot stamping business, is reportedly dependent on Tesla for about 70% of its total revenue. Changes in Tesla's production plans or new model launch schedules could directly affect Myungshin Industry's utilization rate and performance. Indeed, the factory utilization rate declined in 2024 compared to 2023, confirming the volatility associated with dependence on a specific customer.", "8": "The company has cited tariffs and rising costs as causes of the deteriorated performance in 2025. Changes in tariff policies and exchange rate fluctuations in major markets, including the US and China, are variables that can directly affect the cost structure. Given the group's characteristic of having overseas production bases, its exposure to these external variables is relatively high.", "9": "Since converting to a holding company structure in August 2024, MS Autotech has divided its business into the automotive parts business under its subsidiary MS Autosys and the hot stamping-based EV parts business under its listed subsidiary Myungshin Industry. Based on disclosed results, both revenue and operating profit declined for two consecutive years after peaking in 2023, and the company turned to a net loss attributable to controlling shareholders of KRW 108.6 billion in 2025, with net losses attributable to controlling shareholders continuing for five consecutive quarters from Q2 2025 through Q2 2026. However, in Q2 2026, revenue and operating profit improved significantly compared to both the previous quarter and the same period last year, showing signs of a profitability recovery. The subsidiary Myungshin Industry has a structure heavily weighted toward Tesla-bound revenue, and expectations around increased Texas subsidiary revenue and potential new model-related orders have been raised in 2026, though specific order confirmations have not been verified. On the financial side, the debt-to-equity ratio of 166.2% at the end of 2025 and continued reliance on external funding warrant close monitoring of the pace of financial structure improvement. Before making investment decisions, it is important to verify whether the profitability recovery is sustained through upcoming quarterly disclosures and subsidiary-related events. This report is not intended as an investment solicitation and does not include a buy or sell recommendation."}
===JSON_END===

